  "result": "I wrote the revised record to `record.jsonl`. The mappings, compositions, applicability logic, parameters and the other scenarios are the same as before. The only real change is the `branch_2` scenario. Nothing was run here; the gate will check the revision.\n\n**Why `branch_2` returned unknown instead of not_applicable.** The rule excludes the subject when LVMC_11_30_140_B_o1 or _o2 applies. Neither could be settled at the judged instant:\n- **o1 did not apply there.** It applies only at the observation where an approach starts. That means contact begins, or the centre distance first drops to at most `p.blind_person_approach_reach` while the subject is moving. In the coder's trace the pedestrian and the cane user start 27 m apart. The parameter's convention puts the reach at about 32 m (a stopping distance from about 13 m/s with a 1 s reaction time and 4.5 m/s² braking). So the two were within reach from the first scene, and the approach never happened inside the recording. At the last instant there was also no contact, since they were 0.4 m apart.\n- **o2 could not be decided.** It looks back for a past approach, and because that approach would have been before the recording started, the look-back ran off the start of the trace. The result was unknown (`window_truncated:since`), so \"o2 does not apply\" was unknown and so was the whole applicability.\n\n**The fix to the record.** The `branch_2` narrative now spells out what o1 reads, so the coder can build the scene from it:\n- The cane user starts farther than `p.blind_person_approach_reach` from the pedestrian.\n- The judged observation is the first one where the walking pedestrian comes within that reach, having been outside it at the observation before.\n\nAt that point o1 applies, so \"o1 does not apply\" is false, and the applicability is false whatever o2 gives. I also added a short summary of o1's and o2's trigger conditions to the applicability text so the coder has them.",
